(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-risk-ratios-quiz"]={"title":"Risk and Ratios Quiz 1 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What advantage does a retrospective cohort study have over a prospective one?","choices":["It is faster, cheaper and more efficient","It studies a wider range of risk factors","It measures exposure in a standardized way","It can examine any patient characteristic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the data were already collected for another purpose and the outcomes have already occurred; the trade-off is a narrower range of risk factors. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Aspirin raises the risk of upper gastrointestinal bleeding much more in older patients than in younger ones. What is age in this relationship?","choices":["A confounder","The dependent variable","A control group","An effect modifier"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the exposure acts differently depending on age, which is what effect modification means, as opposed to age merely distorting the association. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is effect modification?","choices":["A third factor distorts the association","A variable changes the effect of exposure","Participants drop out during follow-up","Results vary randomly between samples"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the presence or absence of the variable changes the effect of the exposure, and the change may be synergistic or antagonistic. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which bias threatens a case-control study when subjects are asked about past exposure?","choices":["Attrition effect","Publication bias","Recall bias","Non-response bias"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: accuracy of memory and the ability to recall specific information are imperfect, so reported exposure may be inaccurate, especially once subjects know the disease being studied. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Effect modification that weakens the effect of an exposure is called what?","choices":["Antagonistic","Synergistic","Random","Confounding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and effect modification that strengthens the effect of exposure is synergistic; neither form is a bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A study compares patients with newly diagnosed melanoma to patients without it and asks about past sunburns. What is the exposure?","choices":["A diagnosis of melanoma","Being in the control group","Sunburns in the past","The patient's age at diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the exposure is the factor compared between the groups, here past sunburns, while the presence or absence of melanoma defines the groups. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Researchers enroll 150 patients with a rare cancer and 300 without it, and ask about past exposure. Which measure of association suits this study?","choices":["Odds ratio","Relative risk","Attributable risk","Absolute risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the odds ratio is the measure of association for case-control studies, while relative risk is used for cohort studies. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does a case-control study not need to do that a cohort study does?","choices":["Define who counts as a case","Wait for the condition to develop","Identify a comparison group","Ask about earlier exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, since the cases already have the condition, which is why results do not take years to obtain. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does a relative risk below 1 suggest?","choices":["The exposure increases risk","The exposure may be protective","The exposure has no effect","The exposure was measured wrongly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because risk in the exposed is lower than in the unexposed, a negative association, as with protection through exposure to an effective vaccine. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What limits the risk factors a cohort study can examine?","choices":["Only diseases with short latency","Only exposed persons are followed","Only one outcome can be studied","Only exposures recorded at the outset"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so other contributing exposures may be missed when a disease has multiple causes. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does an odds ratio from a case-control study estimate?","choices":["Attributable risk","Absolute risk","Relative risk","Incidence in the exposed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: an odds ratio is an estimated relative risk, which is why the case-control design ends in an estimate of relative risk. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In a case-control study, 60 of 100 cases and 30 of 100 controls had been exposed. What is the odds ratio?","choices":["2.0","3.5","1.5","0.29"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the odds of exposure are 60 to 40, which is 1.5, in cases and 30 to 70, about 0.43, in controls, and the ratio is 3.5, matching ad over bc, 4,200 over 1,200. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What limits the usefulness of a historical cohort study?","choices":["Records may lack needed data points","Outcomes have not yet occurred","Participants cannot be identified","Exposure is assigned by chance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, since records were not set up for all the data points the research needs, so data may be incomplete until the study has run long enough. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which analysis method uses mathematical modeling to adjust for a large number of factors?","choices":["Multivariable adjustment","Simple adjustment","Stratification","Restriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it adjusts for differences in many factors related to the outcome using mathematical modeling techniques, in the analysis phase. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A drug lowers blood pressure far more in younger than in older adults. What does age represent here?","choices":["A confounder","An effect modifier","A source of recall bias","A matched control"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the effect of the exposure differs according to the level of another variable, which is effect modification and not a bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is a risk factor?","choices":["A factor that guarantees later disease","A factor that appears only after disease begins","A treatment that lowers the chance of disease","A factor tied to a higher chance of later disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because it raises the probability of developing the disease later rather than making it certain, which is why risk factors describe groups and not a guaranteed outcome for one person. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"In a cohort 2x2 table with exposed cases (a), exposed noncases (b), unexposed cases (c) and unexposed noncases (d), which expression gives the relative risk?","choices":["ad divided by bc","a/(a+c) divided by b/(b+d)","a/(a+b) divided by c/(c+d)","a/c divided by b/d"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the incidence in the exposed, a over a plus b, divided by the incidence in the unexposed, c over c plus d. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How is incidence, or absolute risk, calculated?","choices":["New cases in a period divided by group size","All current cases divided by group size","New cases divided by all current cases","Exposed cases divided by unexposed cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and because it does not compare exposed with unexposed, absolute risk does not show whether the exposure is associated with increased risk. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What must a cohort study have so that attrition does not falsely skew the outcome?","choices":["Complete follow-up of the cohort","A larger control group","Random assignment to exposure","Recall of past exposures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: if participants drop out, the outcome data can be falsely skewed, so the cohort must also be followed long enough to observe the expected outcome. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What defines a cohort?","choices":["Cases matched with healthy controls","Patients randomly assigned to treatment","A single patient followed in detail","People sharing a trait, followed over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the shared characteristic may be age, date of birth, an exposure, having a specific disease, or taking part in a preventive or therapeutic intervention. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A clinician advises a patient to stop smoking to lower the chance of lung cancer. Which use of risk information is this?","choices":["Prediction","Diagnosis","Cause","Prevention"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because removing a risk factor lowers the probability of developing the disease, as with controlling hypertension to prevent heart failure or avoiding second-hand smoke. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"In which direction does a case-control study look for exposure?","choices":["Forward from exposure to disease","Forward from random assignment","Only at the moment of diagnosis","Backward from disease to past exposure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: cases and controls are identified first and then compared for earlier exposure, so the research runs backward in time and yields an estimate of relative risk. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which method gives each patient an equal chance of falling into either group?","choices":["Restriction","Randomization","Matching","Stratification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: it is applied in the design phase and spreads confounders between the groups by chance. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"In a cohort, 12 of 300 exposed and 16 of 400 unexposed persons developed a disease. What does this show?","choices":["Exposure lowers the risk by a quarter","Exposure raises the risk by a third","No association with the exposure","The counts are insufficient to judge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: 12 of 300 and 16 of 400 are both 0.04, so the relative risk is 1.0, the null value, which indicates no association between exposure and disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which type of cohort study identifies the cohort from records and follows it forward to the present?","choices":["Historical (retrospective) cohort","Concurrent (prospective) cohort","Case-control study","Randomized controlled trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the cohort is assembled in the past from records and followed forward to the present, so the outcomes have already occurred. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which situation makes a risk easiest to recognize?","choices":["A small risk with a long latency","A rare disease with delayed onset","A large risk with rapid effects","A common disease with many known risk factors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because a big effect that appears soon after exposure is hard to miss. Long latency, low incidence and many already-known risk factors each make a risk harder to spot. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which is an advantage of cohort studies?","choices":["Rare diseases can be studied cheaply","Incidence can be established directly","Results are available within weeks","Few participants need enrolling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because following exposed and unexposed persons forward shows how many develop disease; exposure is also recorded without knowledge of the outcome. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why do case-control studies need smaller samples than cohort studies?","choices":["Controls are not required for the comparison","Cases are picked for having the condition","Exposure is randomly assigned to subjects","Bias from recall of exposure is removed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a cohort must be large because only a limited number of those followed get the condition, whereas a case-control study starts with people who already have it. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does attributable risk measure?","choices":["How many times likelier exposed persons are","Incidence in the whole population","Excess incidence in the exposed group","Odds of exposure among cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is the incidence in exposed persons minus the incidence in non-exposed persons, the possibility of disease following exposure. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which method compares rates within subgroups that have a similar probability of the outcome?","choices":["Restriction","Multivariable adjustment","Matching","Stratification"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: comparing rates within strata of otherwise similar risk is an analysis-phase method for controlling confounding. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
